# Yubico release-signing public key — used to verify the yubihsm-shell source
# tarball the `-yubihsm` container image builds from.
#
#   Primary key: 1D73 08B0 055F 5AEF 3694  4A8F 27A9 C24D 9588 EA0F
#   Owner:       Aveen Ismail <aveen.ismail@yubico.com>
#
# Listed under "developers who are currently releasing code" on Yubico's
# software-signing page, which is the authority for this file:
#
#   https://developers.yubico.com/Software_Projects/Software_Signing.html
#
# Vendored rather than fetched from a keyserver at build time: a keyserver
# round-trip is a network dependency that fails intermittently and, worse, one
# whose answer nobody checks — `gpg --recv-keys <id>` will happily import
# whatever key carries that id. The fingerprint is pinned in the Dockerfile
# (ARG YUBIHSM_SHELL_SIGNING_KEY) and asserted against what this file actually
# contains, so editing this file alone cannot change which key is trusted.
#
# Exported with --export-options export-minimal: the certification signatures
# from other people's keys are what make a full export 60 kB, and nothing here
# consults a web of trust. Refresh with:
#
#   gpg --keyserver keys.openpgp.org \
#       --recv-keys 1D7308B0055F5AEF36944A8F27A9C24D9588EA0F
#   gpg --armor --export --export-options export-minimal \
#       1D7308B0055F5AEF36944A8F27A9C24D9588EA0F
#
# Leading comment lines are ignored by gpg, which scans for the armor header.

